From 669cab9c81a38b6b8e5055c252264cec4a41e232 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenWCH Phase 6 <phase6@openwch.local>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 16:33:35 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] RISC-V: add the WCH XW compressed byte and halfword
 accesses

WCH's QingKe cores add eight 16-bit compressed byte/halfword load and
store forms.  gas accepts them as explicit mnemonics and also selects
them for ordinary lbu/lhu/sb/sh whose operands fit, which is where the
code-size difference against the vendor assembler comes from: the
compiler emits no XW instruction of its own, the assembler picks the
short encoding exactly as it does for RVC.

Observed behaviour (vendor 8.2.0 as,
tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s1/probes/raw/05-xw-encodings):

  c.lbu   a0,0(a1)  8821    c.lbusp a0,0(sp)  0880
  c.lhu   a0,0(a1)  8a21    c.lhusp a0,0(sp)  2880
  c.sb    a0,0(a1)  88a1    c.sbsp  a0,0(sp)  4880
  c.sh    a0,0(a1)  8aa1    c.shsp  a0,0(sp)  6880

The four register forms take over the Q0 and Q2 double-precision slots
(c.fld/c.fldsp/c.fsd/c.fsdsp) and the sp forms use the reserved Q0
funct3=100 slot, selecting the operation with bits 6-5.  The immediate
field widths are pinned by the vendor assembler's accept/reject
boundaries in the same probe set: c.lbu takes 31 and rejects 32, c.lhu
takes 62 and rejects 64 and 1, c.lbusp takes 15 and rejects 16.  Field
placement follows ref/wch-isa-research/isa/custom/wch-custom-isa-reference.md
section 3.1, which also records that the sp halfword offset is not a
linear low-bit mapping: uimm[1..3] goes to bits 8-10 and uimm[4] to bit 7.

Because the encodings overlap the D slots, the two sets cannot be live
at once.  The vendor assembler resolves this with a pair of complementary
match functions over one global flag rather than a new instruction class
-- its as and objdump export exactly match_with_wch_rvc_extension,
match_without_wch_rvc_extension and wch_rvc_extension and nothing else
(tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s1/optsweep/optsweep.md sections 3.2 and
3.3).  This reproduces that shape: the XW entries carry the C subset and
the with-flag matcher, the four c.f{ld,sd}{,sp} entries and their fld/fsd
aliases carry the without-flag matcher, and riscv_set_arch raises the flag
when the parsed architecture names both xw and c.  The flag stays clear in
the disassembler, so default objdump output is unchanged and still decodes
these words through the D entries.

Inside gas riscv_set_arch is the only writer of that flag, and it only ever
raises it: nothing lowers it, so a later .attribute arch naming an
architecture without xw does not hand the D slots back.  That is the vendor
behaviour as well, not an omission here.  With .attribute arch,"rv32imac_xw"
followed by .attribute arch,"rv32imafdc", a following c.fld is rejected by
the vendor assembler and by this one alike, under every -march tried
(tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s4/defect-fidelity/out/*/raw/
19-attr-arch-monotonic: four sources -- no directive, xw only, imafdc only,
xw then imafdc -- each assembled under -march=rv32imafdc, rv32imac and
rv32imafdc_xw, 12 invocations, derived table monotonic.tsv; the two sides
agree case by case).  A reset path would therefore be a behaviour change,
not a cleanup.

Reading the parsed subset list rather than riscv_subset_supports is what
splits the two diagnostics the vendor assembler produces, and both were
measured: without c in -march the mnemonic is skipped for want of the
compressed class and gas reports "unrecognized opcode"
(tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s1/probes/raw/05-xw-encodings/
gating-correction), while ".option rvc" makes the class available so the
entry is considered and the failing matcher downgrades it to "illegal
operands" (tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s1/probes/raw/05-xw-encodings/
gating.tsv, 27 cases).  .option rvc must not by itself select XW.

Operand characters: the vendor as carries four argument strings that
stock 2.32 does not -- "Ct,Cb(Cs)", "Ct,CB(Cs)", "Ct,Ch(Cc)" and
"Ct,CH(Cc)", extracted from its __TEXT,__cstring.  The (Cs)/(Cc) split
fixes b/B as the register forms and h/H as the sp forms; lower and upper
case then separate byte from halfword within each pair, which is not
separately observable.  All four sub-characters are free in 2.32's C
namespace.  xlen_requirement is 0 rather than 32 because the vendor
assembler also accepts these under -march=rv64imacxw -mabi=lp64,
emitting the same 8821 (measured).

gas/testsuite: 13 new tests, every expected byte and diagnostic taken
from a vendor-assembler run of the same source
(tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s3/d2/spec-probe): xw-compressed, xw-imm,
xw-imm-fail, xw-gate-no-c, xw-gate-no-xw, xw-gate-option-rvc, xw-dslot,
xw-dslot-fail, xw-dslot-nozw, xw-norvc, xw-attribute, xw-no-compress,
xw-gate-attr-only.
